WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:26.000
The water into blood, but they were never able to turn the blood back into water. You understand? Now, Moshe Rabbenu, total control of nature, alright, and is able to switch in both directions, and therefore is not only able to bring the plague, but also to remove the plague.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:55.000
One Akram makes a very interesting observation. He says, all the wisdom of the ancients, magic, yes, is in the power to do evil. And therefore you are afraid of the god, because the god can destroy, bring plagues, I don't know, catastrophes, sickness, but the same gods who are so mighty in bringing evil into the world, are unable to cure anything.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:12.000
For Alam, not only brings the Mecca, but he is the one who removes the Mecca. Alright? Total power, total control. We have still a few minutes. Many other questions.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:27.000
Last week, we heard that Alam asked that every lady or every man can take gold and silver from the Muslims, right? Yes.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:45.000
Now, we didn't know that that gold was going to be a sword that one day they were going to put it on the fire and make the golden gold. Yes, but this is what is important.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:02:11.000
This is what I call, Alam gives us freedom of choice for everything. Let me explain. We ask for Alam for wealth. Alam gives us the wealth. It's up to us what to do with it. It doesn't mean because god knows the future that he prevents us from having opportunities.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:28.000
Now, the same thing, god gave us the wealth. Because we did not use it for good, we are going to use it for bad. It is up to us what to do with the gift of Alam. In other words, we have children.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:48.000
Now, it doesn't mean because we could turn our children in different directions that Alam is not going to give us children. He gives us and we have the responsibility. It is up to us to do with whatever Alam gave us and to direct it into something good.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:03:08.000
In other words, the gold that god gave us, it could have been used for the bitamikdash, but we didn't use it for good. We wanted to use it for bad. It is up to us. Some of the people use the gold, as this gentleman said very correctly, to build the golden gate.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:32.000
I have a word in English. It's a little bit fancy, but I call it the bifurcation of the road. Wherever you are in Judaism, the road bifurcates, turns into two directions. It is up to you to choose between the right and wrong. There is no such a thing, a situation, in which there is no bifurcation of the road.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:50.000
Always the road splits, and it is up to you to do it for good or to do it for bad. That's part of the miracles of this. Now, still we have a couple of minutes, two or three minutes. I want to give an address so we become punctual, and I want to thank all, you know, go ahead.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:04:12.000
Last week, parasha Bo, he should have said, we are the old Mizran Kenyashim. Why are we the old Mizran Kenyashim? Why are we the old Mizran Kenyashim for them, not for the Mizran?

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:33.000
All right. The point is this. All that God did to the Egyptians is marginal for the Egyptians. The only people who are going to learn a lesson of what happened to the Egyptians, as we are going to see today in today's parasha, are not the Egyptians, are the Jewish people.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:49.000
In Hebrew, to know, there are three words for knowledge. We say it every day in the Amilat. Chokmah, Bina, Vada. Yes? Chokmah, Bina, Vada. Let me tell you what the difference between one and the other is.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:05:10.000
Chokmah is the knowledge of a professor, a scientist, in abstract. He sits down, Einstein in his room, and he figures out the law of relativity. Fine, very good. It's a total abstract idea. He learned it just by figuring out things in abstract. That is chokmah.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:33.000
Bina is the application of abstract theories, Einstein, into concrete reality. Out of this theory, somebody figured out how to fuse the atom and use it for hydraulic energy, to produce, I don't know, electricity. This is bina.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:56.000
And then there is something which is different, is dahat. Dahat is the knowledge that you get through your experience. Vayela adam et haba eshtoh. Means, through carnal, intimate, personal contact. This is the knowledge that we gain through experience.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:25.000
Now, interestingly enough, the knowledge of God that you have in abstract is not a very powerful knowledge. Do you know what is the strongest knowledge of God? Bidaatim. Through your experience, by seeing what happened in Egypt, you're going to know God, not as an abstract knowledge, as a philosopher who writes many books, but as a person, as an individual who lived through it.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:42.000
And this is what God made a Jew. Something special. And this is the Hakhamim they said. Haramdam said it, Yudalevi said it, Rav Nisim Gaon said it, Rav She'alia Gaon said it. This is the difference between the knowledge that the Jew has of God and what the Goi has of God.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:07:04.000
The knowledge that the Goi has of God is an abstract knowledge. He sits down, he figures out, somehow there should be a God. Fine, very nice. The knowledge that we have is a knowledge that we gain throughout our experience. It's a different quality of knowledge. Do you understand?

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:24.000
Therefore, in Judaism, you don't need to be an intellectual to believe in God. Now I think we're going to begin with an extraordinary perasha. It is perhaps the greatest physical miracle in this planet's history. And this is the splitting of the Red Sea.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:50.000
Now, it's page 265 in the Lodge for Mashiah. Now, let's read. And it came to pass, page 265, when Pharaoh sent the people.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:08:18.000
Now, there are two roads from Egypt into the Holy Land. One road, which is, you go to the Mediterranean, you go north, yes, you go north to the Mediterranean, then you follow the Mediterranean coast, and in a few days, March, you are where?

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:33.000
You are where? In Israel. So that is called what? Derek Erez Philistine. Very simple. From Egypt, you go by the seashore, you travel north, in a very short distance, you're going to be in Israel.

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:58.000
Instead of going this way, God did not send the Jewish people through the road of the land of the Philistines, which is through Erez Yisrael, because it's too near. We're going to see. It's going to be near, and therefore, if they're going to be near, they have to conquer Israel on their own, and God says somehow it's no good. We're going to see why.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:21.000
Alright? Yiamal Elohim, because God said, Elinahima ambir otam el-hamah, because the people, if they will see, they will have to face war, they're going to change their heart, there's going to be a change of heart, Beshavu Mesraima, and then they will want to return where? To Egypt.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:36.000
Now, this is an extraordinary verse, and we have to explain it word by word. As a matter of fact, letter by letter. More precise, dot by dot. There is a dot here, that makes absolutely no sense.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:54.000
The first, the second word, Beshalah. Now, there is a dot, the lambda. And we know in Hebrew, there are two tenses, two manners of saying a verb. One is Kal, and the other is Fi'al.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:10:10.000
Fi'al means, when you have a dagesh inside, Fi'al means, that's something that was done by force, with strength. Shalah means he sent away. Shalah means he sent away somebody against his will.

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:30.000
Shalah in Hebrew, that's Fi'al, that means send somebody against his will. The mother doesn't want the pigeon, says you send her against his will. That is Fi'al, it means against the will.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:47.000
Now, here it says, when Pharaoh changed the way, expelled the Jewish people, Shalah means, what does it mean? Not send away means Fi'al. What does it mean? Shalah, against the will.

00:10:47.000 --> 00:11:03.000
Does it mean that the Jewish people didn't want it to go? We have all the miracles and so on, what does it mean? Beshalah. This is a very profound lesson. I was talking to a gentleman before the class.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:28.000
I believe that every Jew, if he's attentive, will realize that the Tora is not a book of the past, but rather is a book of the present, speaking to us about our affairs today. And this is really, you're going to understand this in context of what's going on with us today.

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:53.000
The Hakhamim explained that during the ten plagues, there were many Jews who did not believe in Moses, who did not believe in God, and moreover, did not want to go to the Holy Land. They wanted to remain in Egypt.

00:11:53.000 --> 00:12:10.000
There is a Pesah. I was studying, we read it last week. It says about the Qorban Pesah. The Qorban Pesah says, everybody may eat it. And then it gives you the conditions. It says, Behol ben nechar, lo yohal bom.

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:28.000
Now, what does it mean ben nechar? The superficial translation would be a foreigner, an alien, a goat. Somebody who is a goat should not eat it. But that's strange, since when did a goat want to eat the Qorban Pesah? I'm talking about Egypt.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:50.000
The Hakhamim came to explain, you know what it means ben nechar? An apostate Jew. A Jew who does not want anything to do with Judaism. And there is a proof to this. This is the Pesah of the Pesah. Because the Pesah doesn't say, Behol nochli lo yohal bom.

00:12:50.000 --> 00:13:08.000
But says, ben nechar. Ben nechar means somebody who has been influenced by the lochiyud. He's not an alien. He's like, even an alien. You know what it means, if you understand Arabic. Hebrew means somebody who is influenced by alienation.

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:30.000
In other words, in Egypt there were people who were Jews, who were ben nechar. They were assimilated to the culture of the land. They believed in those values, in those key issues, in the same ideas as the Egyptians.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:52.000
And for them, to go out from Egypt, it was no good. That's why the Pesah says, v'eibesh allah paro et aham. Some people will tell you, in the last Pesah they left Egypt. But when the crisis came, paro decided to send away everybody.

00:13:52.000 --> 00:14:12.000
Every Jew goes. And there were those Jews who they had to be chased away by force. V'eibesh allah paro et aham. And on our own days. Let's talk about one minute ago in history. One minute ago in history. You know what was the one minute ago in history?

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:34.000
Germany. Fifty years ago. Many Jews, they were so assimilated that they believed Germany is our Israel and Berlin our Yerushalayim. Under no circumstances they will second-rate Germany.

