And eventually, God wants them to reach to the Red Sea. Now, this seems, therefore, that the Jewish people were what? Totally disorganized. To go to Israel, you want to go to Israel from Egypt. Instead of going north-east, you go south-west. It means that what? Totally disorganized.

00:21:42.000 --> 00:22:04.000
A bunch of what? Of running away slaves, not knowing where they're going, not knowing how to conduct themselves in the desert. V'hamushim. The Lord said, don't be fooled. This is appearances. There is a purpose for this appearance. Alright? There is a purpose.

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:23.000
Really, v'hamushim alu b'nei Yisrael. The Jewish people came out marching like a troop. Hamush in Hebrew means somebody who is walking with military precision. In other words, here the Tora is playing now the scenario very nicely.

00:22:23.000 --> 00:22:51.000
On the one hand, if you see it from the outside, long shot, it seems what? A bunch of slaves, scrambled, lost, without no direction. And another, you look at them and you're going to realize that these people are not crazy. Are really very highly disciplined. V'hamushim. Hamush means in Hebrew, walking with military precision.

00:22:51.000 --> 00:23:10.000
V'hamushim alu b'nei Yisrael. Now, look at the level of discipline. Yosef ha-Saddiq, 210 years before that event, made an oath with the Jewish people. Do you remember the oath? Take my bones.

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:29.000
In the middle of all this, v'yikach Moshe et asmot Yosef and more. Moses remembered the oath and he took the bones of Yosef with him. Why? Ki ha'shveah ha'shveah alu b'nei Yisrael and more. Because he made the Jewish people an oath.

00:23:29.000 --> 00:23:57.000
Now, two Hakhamim Sepharadim, beautiful, they say something extraordinary. The name of them is Rabi Yoel Ibn Shu'ayb. He was the Tamid of the Rishvah in Spain.

