But literally, Moshe didn't bury Yosef. Yes, he was the Asak of the Rishvah. Yes, but he was the Asak of the Rishvah. He couldn't do more because he didn't come to the Rishvah.

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:27.000
Now, one minute. They left Sukkot. Sukkot is the boundary city of Egypt. The last city in Egypt. In the south. The last city was called Sukkot.

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:48.000
Now, they were going to go into the desert. Now, very important thing. You know why Moshe Rabbenu always said, we want to go to the desert? Moshe said, I want to go to the desert. I want to go three days into the desert.

00:25:48.000 --> 00:26:06.000
You know why? Some people say, oh, Moshe Rabbenu misled Pharaoh. Because he said, you want to go to the desert means that you want to come back. No. In Hebrew, the word mitvah, that not only means desert, but means a no man's land.

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:27.000
A land that is under the jurisdiction of no one. And you know why is it so important? Because Pharaoh had jurisdiction where? In Egypt. When you say, I want to go out into the desert, you know what he's saying? I want to be out of what? Of your jurisdiction.

00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:52.000
So now, as soon as they leave into the desert, they no longer under the jurisdiction of whom? Of Pharaoh. As long as you are in this United States of America, always the president or whatever authority could say, I'm sorry, we revoke the permit. Understand? Once that you're out, then it has no more right.

00:26:52.000 --> 00:27:14.000
And therefore, they reached a place called Etam. Right? And they were, you cross this point, and where are you going to be? Outside the jurisdiction of Egypt. Now, as they're going, and the Egyptians are thinking that we are going to the desert, we're going to die. Yes?

00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:38.000
We were organized. Not only within, but we were walking with military precision, but also from the outside. God was marching with the Jewish people. You know the difference between yom and yom? Who knows Hebrew well? What's the difference between yom and yom?

00:27:38.000 --> 00:28:01.000
Yom means a day. A day includes day and night. Yom means when the sun is on. Yom includes Layla in Hebrew. But yom means when the sun is on.

00:28:01.000 --> 00:28:25.000
So therefore, yomam, during the sun, daytime, be'amud anam. God was leading them with a pillar of cloud. And this pillar of cloud will direct them into the road. You know how we march? We just follow the pillar of cloud.

00:28:25.000 --> 00:28:50.000
But not only we travel during the day. It was a seven-day march. Non-stop, also at night. Be'Layla, and at night, this pillar of cloud was switched, changed into a pillar of fire. Le'ha'illahem, to shine for us, to light into the road.

00:28:51.000 --> 00:29:10.000
In this way, we walk, we march at night and during the day. It was a very fast traveling. And there was a reason for it. We wanted to reach where? Yamsu. We wanted to reach the Red Sea. And there's a reason why.

00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:29.000
And never cease to shine, to illuminate the pillar of cloud during the day. Or the pillar of fire at night. Always was directing the people where to go.

00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:46.000
Now, as we say, the people reached where? Etam. And when they reached Etam, which was the last place in Egypt, now par'o gets the news. He had a messenger service. We don't know how. Maybe through signs.

00:29:46.000 --> 00:30:05.000
And then Moshe is going to know about it. Par'o is going to know about it. And then you're going to know what happened. And you're going to see something very interesting. By the bed of Moshe Lemor. And God says to Moses, the bed of Israel, tell the Jewish people, they are Shuvu, and they should come back.

00:30:05.000 --> 00:30:24.000
They are going to come back. They go southwest, and they are going to return back. They are Shuvu. And they are going to return northeast. Northeast. They are Shuvu.

00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:45.000
They are Hanu. And let them camp. In a place called Piyahiyot. Let me tell you about this Piyahiyot. Piyahiyot is like a small little peninsula. An ismus. An ismus is, there's like an island, from three sides is water, and from the other side is land.

00:30:46.000 --> 00:31:13.000
Now, let us assume here is the Red Sea. Alright? From three sides is the Red Sea. That's called Piyahiyot. Alright? And from the north, from here, from this side, there is a mountain. And let the Jewish people go and scramble themselves and all pay themselves well in this little ismus. Piyahiyot is an ismus. I-S-T-H-M-U-S. It's like a little island from three sides.

00:31:13.000 --> 00:31:30.000
Yes, it's not an island. It's actually like a small little peninsula. And let them all camp, yes, three million or three and a half, into this little piece of land, see from all the sides, and from the north, I'm talking about from this side, the mountain. Alright?

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:43.000
Well, we don't know yet. It's tension. This is Monday. You read the script. You must have read the whole book. Alright? Alright? Now.

