Now, the amal par'on in Israel. And par'on would say about the Jewish people, nebuchim and ba'ales. They are lost in the land. They don't know where they're going. These people have no traveling experience. They are not great sailors. They don't know how to navigate. They don't know how to go from place to place.

00:33:17.000 --> 00:33:44.000
Zagar, alayam, hamizbad. The desert. Trap them. And then, I'm going to make par'on pay for all the evil that he did to the Jewish people. Ve'hezzakti et par'on. I don't give him freedom of choice to this person. I'm going to make his heart hard. You know, strong.

00:33:45.000 --> 00:33:59.000
Ve'hadafah alayam. And he's going to run, pursue them. And then when they're going to, he's going to try to kill the Jewish people, the kabedave par'on. I'm going to show my glory through par'on.

00:33:59.000 --> 00:34:18.000
Uko'he'lom. Uko'he'lom, who threw out all his army. Ve'hadah, almost trying. And then, at that moment, as they're dying, they shall know, ki'ani adonai. And now, there's a great virtue to the Jewish people, ve'yaha sukhem. And the Jewish people obey.

00:34:18.000 --> 00:34:39.000
You know, what does it mean to let yourself, in the middle of the desert, to let yourself into a little small peninsula, three sides is the sea, and the other side is Begdor, a military position, and then you go, and now with faith, and you say, yes.

00:34:40.000 --> 00:34:58.000
Ve'yaha sukhem. It was an admirable, a great situation, a great misrah, zekhut, to be able to listen to God, that was a great zekhut. Ve'yaha sukhem. And immediately, vayugat l'Melekh misraim ki bara ha'am.

